===== ISSUE 18 =====
title:	Thumbs up/down feedback that weights model-vs-metrics fit
state:	OPEN
author:	alex-moon
labels:	enhancement
comments:	0
assignees:	
projects:	
milestone:	
number:	18
--
Users currently have no way to say whether a reply was good. This ticket: thumbs up/down on assistant messages, which "weights" (adjusts confidence in) the selected model's fit for the metrics collected per message (issue #17 — dependency).

Suggested approach:
- API: `POST /api/messages/:messageId/feedback` with `{ value: 'up' | 'down' }`, authenticated, idempotent (revote just overwrites); persists on the message document alongside its `metrics` from #17.
- Frontend: thumbs up/down pair under each assistant message, subtle pending/opt-out lift, already-visible messages get the controls too.
- Weighting model: keep a `modelMetricsStats` store (Mongo collection) of per-(modelName, metric-band) counts → up/down tallies, e.g. `llama-3.3-70b + high-technical + long prompt → 12↑/3↓`. When the router scores candidate models for a message, factor these empirical fit scores in alongside the existing complexity heuristics.
- Cold start: fall back to neutral weight until a minimum sample count per band.
- Feeding the router: this complements the online router, not replaces it; log the applied weight in the routing reason so LangSmith (#5) can surface it.
- Acceptance: feedback round-trips for authed users (401 for others); thumbs show their current state after reload; router decisions demonstrably shift with accumulated feedback (covered by a test seeding stats and asserting a different model choice).
===== ISSUE 17 =====
title:	Collect per-message prompt metrics (classifier features)
state:	OPEN
author:	alex-moon
labels:	enhancement
comments:	0
assignees:	
projects:	
milestone:	
number:	17
--
We want to understand *why* the router picks the model it picks, and later whether that choice was right (feedback ticket reads these). This ticket: collect a set of metrics per chat message and persist them alongside the message document in MongoDB.

Metrics to collect — a mix of cheap/deterministic (computed synchronously, no LLM):
- word count / char count / short-vs-long bucket
- average word length and syllable-ish estimate (vowel-cluster heuristic is fine) → "complexity of vocabulary" proxy
- code-y indicator (fences, backticks, code-ish tokens)
- share of technical jargon vs plain words (stopword ratio as cheap proxy)
- message index within the session (how many messages into the chat we are)
- presence of question mark / imperative verbs (question vs instruction)

And LLM-classified dimensions (one cheap small-model classification pass per message, cached, run in parallel with routing):
- emotional vs neutral
- technical vs lay
- high-level/strategic vs low-level/detailed
- positive vs negative sentiment
- open-ended vs closed/one-shot task

Suggested approach:
- Deterministic metrics: pure TS module, no deps, unit-tested.
- LLM dimensions: reuse the small/guard model path from the router; single structured-output call producing the flags, degraded gracefully (nulls) on failure.
- Persist as a `metrics` subdocument on each message record so routing decisions and future feedback can be correlated.
- Acceptance: every new assistant/user turn gets metrics filled; deterministic metrics pass unit tests; no visible latency added to first token (classification is fire-and-forget or batched post-stream).
===== ISSUE 16 =====
title:	Web search tool for the agent loop
state:	OPEN
author:	alex-moon
labels:	enhancement
comments:	0
assignees:	
projects:	
milestone:	
number:	16
--
Today the chat pipeline (`/api/chat/stream` → `routerService.routeQuery` → one Groq completion) has no tool access, and issue #4 proposes the agentic loop that would host one. This ticket: the first tool — web search — implemented in Mastra.

Suggested approach:
- Add Mastra as the tool layer for the server pipeline; define a `webSearch` tool with a typed schema (query string, optional freshness/recency filter).
- Wire the tool into the agentic loop from #4 (stop budget / max iterations carried over the existing SSE `event:` channel so the UI can show ` Searching the web...` status).
- Provider choice: use a search API with a simple REST client (e.g. Brave/Tavily/Exa), keyed by env (`SEARCH_API_KEY`), disabled cleanly when unset so local dev keeps working.
- Synthesis: pipe results (title/snippet/url) back into the main model as context for the final answer, keep citations in the assistant message payload so the frontend can render sources later.
- Persistence: one assistant message per turn stays true; tool internals are trace artifacts, not separate messages.
- Acceptance: a question requiring fresh info gets grounded answers with sources attached; tool runs emit SSE status events; tests mock the search provider without breaking existing suites.
